{% comment %}
  Full view — 800x480 on an OG, 1040x780 on a TRMNL X. Six pair rows with
  sparklines, eight on the X. Merge variables come from the Worker's /rates
  response.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
    TREND takes the free width (1fr) and HI / LO sizes to its content (auto).
    The reverse (a fixed sparkline with 1fr on the bounds) left ~119px of
    empty space inside the last cell, stranding the numbers at the far edge.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE TYPE SCALE
    The rate is the number the panel exists to show, so it is the largest thing
    on it: `value--base` (38px), with the pair at `value--small` (26px) beside
    it, the two tiers a clear step apart so the row reads pair-then-rate rather
    than as one line of bold. The change and the bounds step down again, to
    `label` and `label--small`. This is the v2 design critique's hierarchy on
    the framework's own ladder, adopted at the cost of sparkline width: the
    floors below are measured to these tiers (see shared.liquid on why they
    are floors), and the TREND track is what is left.

    On a TRMNL X the rate steps up to `lg:value--large` and the change to
    `lg:label--large`; the pair and the bounds stay where they are, because
    every px the fixed columns take comes off the trace, and the X's extra
    width is better spent on wider traces than on a larger name beside a
    rate that already dominates. The tracks under `.screen--lg` are measured
    to those tiers. In portrait the X is only 780px wide, so the
    `lg:portrait:` tiers drop back to the OG ladder and the OG tracks apply;
    the extra height goes to the plots instead.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    ROW HEIGHT BUDGET
    The rate's line box sets the row: `value--base` is 42px tall and the row
    inset is `py--2.5` (10px each side), so 6 rows x (42 + 20 + 1px rule)
    plus ~85px of header + title_bar chrome fills the OG panel to within 2px.
    On the X, `value--large` is a 70px line box and the inset drops to
    `lg:py--1` (4px) so that eight of them still clear the title bar:
    8 x (70 + 8 + 1) + 85 = 717 of 780.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    The plot height is (panel - chrome) / rows - inset, in the custom
    properties below so each breakpoint can restate one term: --fx-chrome is
    the header, its rule and the title_bar (8px more on the X, where the
    header is the 16px `label` tier), --fx-inset the row's padding plus its
    1px rule, --fx-rows the rows that breakpoint shows. The result matches
    the rate's line box to within 2px, so the plot and the type set the row
    height together.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %} Rows a large screen shows: the plot height below divides by it. {% endcomment %}
{% assign fx_rows_lg = rows | size | at_most: 8 | at_least: 1 %}
<style>
  .fx--full .fx-row,
  .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px minmax(208px, auto) 86px 1fr minmax(82px, auto); gap: 20px; }
  .screen--lg .fx--full .fx-row,
  .screen--lg .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px minmax(316px, auto) 92px 1fr minmax(88px, auto); }
  .screen--portrait .fx--full .fx-row,
  .screen--portrait .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px minmax(208px, auto) 86px 1fr minmax(82px, auto); }
  .fx--full { --fx-top-pad: 10px; --fx-chrome: 85px; --fx-inset: 21px; --fx-rows: 6; }
  .screen--lg .fx--full { --fx-chrome: 93px; --fx-inset: 9px; --fx-rows: {{ fx_rows_lg }}; }
  .screen--lg.screen--portrait .fx--full { --fx-inset: 21px; }
  .fx--full .fx-dot  { r: 4.5px; }
  .screen--lg .fx--full .fx-dot { r: 6px; }
  .fx--full .fx-spark svg { height: calc((var(--screen-h) - var(--fx-chrome) - var(--fx-top-pad)) / var(--fx-rows) - var(--fx-inset)); }
</style>

<div class="layout layout--col layout--justify-start">
  {% if fx_failed %}
    <div class="fx-error flex flex--col flex--center-y text--center">
      <span class="title">Rates unavailable</span>
      <span class="label label--gray 1bit:text--black">
        {{ error | default: "No data returned" }}
      </span>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <div class="fx-list flex flex--col gap--none pt--2.5 fx--full">
      {% comment %}
        The window names the TREND column but governs three of them: the change
        figure, the sparkline and hi/lo are all measured over it. It sits here
        rather than in the title bar because a column header is read with the
        numbers, which is what keeps CHANGE from being taken for a daily move.
      {% endcomment %}
      <div class="fx-head grid pb--1">
        <span class="label label--small text--bold lg:label--base">PAIR</span><span class="label label--small text--bold lg:label--base text--right">LATEST</span><span class="label label--small text--bold lg:label--base text--right">CHANGE</span><span class="label label--small text--bold lg:label--base text--center">{% if range_label %}{{ range_label }} {% endif %}TREND</span><span class="label label--small text--bold lg:label--base text--right">HI / LO</span>
      </div>

      {% comment %}
        EIGHT ROWS ON A LARGE SCREEN, SIX ELSEWHERE
        The field allows eight pairs. Six rows of 38px rate fill a TRMNL OG;
        the TRMNL X has 300px more height, so it shows all eight. Rows seven
        and eight are `hidden` and `lg:grid`, the framework's own breakpoint
        classes, so only a large screen displays them; the plot height above
        divides by the rows a large screen actually has, so six pairs on an X
        still fill it. Portrait X is also `lg` and taller still.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% for row in rows limit: 8 %}
        <div class="fx-row grid py--2.5 lg:py--1 lg:portrait:py--2.5{% if forloop.index > 6 %} hidden lg:grid{% endif %}">
          <div class="fx-pair value value--small text--bold">{{ row.from }}<span class="fx-arrow text--regular px--0.5">&rarr;</span>{{ row.to }}</div>
          <div class="fx-rate value value--base value--tnums text--bold lg:value--large lg:portrait:value--base text--right">{{ row.rate_str }}</div>
          <div class="fx-chg flex flex--right gap--none"><span class="label text--bold lg:label--large lg:portrait:label--base {% if row.change_pct < 0 %}label--filled{% else %}label--outline{% endif %}">{{ row.change_str }}%</span></div>
          {% assign fx_end = row.points | split: " " | last | split: "," %}
          {% assign fx_end_y = fx_end[1] | times: 100.0 | divided_by: 30 %}
          <div class="fx-spark flex gap--none">
            <svg class="fx-plot block" width="100%" height="30">
              <svg class="block" viewBox="0 0 200 30" width="100%" height="100%" preserveAspectRatio="none">
                <polyline points="{{ row.points }}" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/>
              </svg>
              <circle class="fx-dot" cx="100%" cy="{{ fx_end_y }}%"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="fx-range flex flex--col flex--between gap--none text--right"><span class="label value--tnums">{{ row.hi_str }}</span><span class="label value--tnums">{{ row.lo_str }}</span></div>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

{% comment %}
  Full view: .title_bar comes AFTER .layout. TRMNL's
  `.view--full .layout:has(+.title_bar)` rule reserves the height and adds the
  gap only when the bar is the layout's next sibling.
{% endcomment %}
<div class="title_bar">
  {{ fx_icon }}
  <span class="title">Exchange Rates{% if env %} ({{ env | upcase }}){% endif %}</span>
  <span class="instance">
    {% if fx_failed %}No data{% else %}<span class="fx-dates text--default">{{ fx_dates }}</span> &middot; ECB{% endif %}
  </span>
</div>
